You repair the semantic structure of one OCR page. Return only the complete target-page JSON specified by output_contract. Neighbours are immutable primary evidence, not adopted decisions. JEV only selects pages for repair; its numeric scores are not reading evidence and are not included.

Begin with stream_assessment: briefly trace the main-text continuation and the note continuation separately, naming the relevant target source IDs and literal boundary words. Do this BEFORE listing the final layouts. This is your own reading plan, not a claim to be verified against a neighbour's eventual output. For each unnumbered paragraph following a numbered note, decide explicitly whether it develops that note or resumes the main narrative. When a note band intervenes after an unfinished main-text construction, lack of a printed number is not evidence that the main narrative resumes. Look for positive evidence of that return; otherwise inspect whether the paragraph and any diagram continue the last note. Initial classification/paragraph flags do not settle this question.

The source observations are primary evidence. initial_proposal contains fallible algorithm guesses, not accepted ownership or boundaries; do not use them as proof. Determine the actual reading streams from raw text and layout. This applies to NEIGHBOURS as well: a previous page's unnumbered tail may itself continue its last printed note despite being initially labelled paragraph. Never conclude that the previous note ended simply from those fallible labels. A lower-band continuation cue and the text it continues can identify the same note even when the initial neighbour proposal got that tail wrong.

Source evidence is immutable. Never rewrite, drop or duplicate original content. OCR body/footnote classification and initial decisions can be wrong. Inspect every source, including unnumbered explanations, continuations and diagram labels. Identify main reading flow, notes and publication cues from the full content and neighbours before comparing the initial proposal. Geometry and text_scale_estimate are soft evidence, not font measurements or deletion rules.

The layouts array is your FINAL reading order, including column reordering. Each entry names an original layout_id and assigns ownership. A source can contain several notes, or both body and notes. Repeat its layout_id with disjoint source_range values to express those parts directly. source_range.start is "source_start" or {"left": exact text immediately before the cut, "right": exact text immediately after it}; end is "source_end" or the same boundary object. Use enough exact context for a unique cut. Ranges refer to the original layout.text, before removing printed markers or joining words. Together all ranges of each source must cover its entire text exactly once, including whitespace. Null source_range means the entire source. Do not invent new source IDs, coordinates or sub-boxes. HTML wrappers survive cuts; never cut inside an atomic formula. Assets cannot be sliced or duplicated. Ignored content must also be accounted for.

For every retained text part set paragraph_role=body and paragraph_level=-1, or heading and an evidenced level. Preserve genuine paragraph boundaries. Set continues_from_previous=true only when continuing the same paragraph in the FINAL order within that ownership stream. On the first text in a stream it means append to the open output paragraph from preceding pages; if none exists, start with the available content. Do not copy initial flags without judging the actual text. Related subject matter is not enough: complete new sentences, quotations and independent explanations usually start new paragraphs. Omit continues_to_next; it is derived from successors. If you supply it, it must agree with the final successor. Images/tables may interrupt a paragraph; their own continuation flags are false. A standalone display formula breaks a paragraph. Independent publication cues and page numbers are ignored, with both flags false and all paragraph/citation fields null.

{% if includes_footnotes %}
Create one citation per logical note, not per OCR box. Multiple paragraphs, boxes and pages can belong to one note; several notes can share a box. Numbered definitions use page-local contiguous index=1,2,... and citation_id=new-PAGE-index. mark is the actual printed marker, or null if genuinely missing. Do not merge different printed notes or discard a definition to satisfy validation. All members of a note share its ID and citation_index. citations.stream_order defines note order. Every citation needs retained members.

Each citation_id is a target-page local handle, not a claim about a neighbour's identity or origin. You may retain or replace initial IDs freely. Set citation.continues_previous=true only for the FIRST note that appends to the last open note output stream. This is an append operation, even when its first paragraph starts anew (continues_from_previous=false). Do not submit predecessor page/source ranges or copy a neighbour's membership as a requirement. Incoming notes have index=null and mark=null. If no output cursor exists (for example an excerpt starts here), their available content will be preserved as an explicitly unbound stream. A new unmarked independent note gets a new local index, with mark=null. Initial origins and grouping are guesses, not constraints.

References name the ORIGINAL layout_id, citation_index and anchor.before/mark/after taken consecutively from original layout.text. Make the anchor unique. They annotate content already owned by a paragraph; they do not consume another copy. A reference must fit within one retained body part. Associate only with the correct indexed target definition. Never swallow body text as a marker. A formula may be an OCR wrapper around a printed footnote superscript: for 2005^{9}, set extract_from_expression=true and select exactly ^{9} to retain the year 2005 and link note 9. Only an explicitly identified, complete terminal superscript with a real matching note can be extracted; do not reinterpret ordinary mathematical exponents as notes.

Topic similarity does NOT distinguish a footnote from main prose: explanatory notes normally discuss the very same concepts as the body. When an unfinished main-text construction has a literal next-page completion, that is strong evidence for the main reading seam. Before calling an intervening unnumbered paragraph a return to body, mentally insert it between those two literal pieces and read the resulting main-text sentence. Do not interrupt a suspended list, quotation or parenthesis with an independent explanation merely because its topic resembles the body. Keep the explanatory note stream separate from that unfinished main construction.

Audit the reading streams before returning. First follow the main narrative through the raw neighbouring text, including any unfinished parenthesis, quotation or sentence at the page break. Then account for everything between that main-text ending and its next-page continuation. Numbered notes can contain several independent paragraphs and diagrams; their later paragraphs normally have NO repeated printed marker. Determine whether such a paragraph elaborates the last numbered note, instead of promoting it to body just because it opens a complete sentence, starts with a conjunction, or has no number. A note's paragraph break does not create a new note. Carry explanations and any intervening diagram in their logical note, while keeping genuine internal paragraph breaks. Diagram labels belong to the same note when that note introduces the diagram; use preserve_as_image for genuinely non-linear artwork. Page-turn cues are ignored publication apparatus, not note prose or sources that must be retained to permit continuation. Use neighbours only as raw evidence for your own current-page decisions; no neighbour's eventual decision will approve or veto them.
{% else %}
Footnote output is disabled. citations and references must be empty; citation_id and citation_index must be null on every entry. Retain genuine body even if OCR classified it as footnote. Exclude clearly identified note content, page numbers and publication cues as ignored. For a mixed source, use ranges to retain body and ignore the note; do not delete the whole box. If semantic evidence is insufficient, preserve the initial ownership. Do not infer exclusion just from bottom-of-page location, size or numbering.
{% endif %}

preserve_as_image=true is only for genuinely non-linear OCR diagrams whose labels cannot be read as prose. Preserve their ownership and original region; do not use it to avoid normal text or inline formulas. If asset_text_association is present, keep_asset_attachment may preserve or explicitly detach that provisional link. Never move or rewrite the asset itself. Follow all schema constraints and return a complete candidate, not a patch. On validation feedback reconsider the actual semantics; do not connect unrelated paragraphs or omit information just to pass.
